      "title": "Microsoft Workflow Notification Abuse: QuickBooks Phishing",
      "summary": "A phishing campaign impersonated QuickBooks through legitimate Microsoft Dynamics 365 Customer Voice infrastructure, so SPF, DKIM, and DMARC all passed. The email, displayed as \"Intuit Accountants,\" used a fake \"enhanced Passkey\" update and a 48-hour deadline to push recipients to a verification button. That button led through a benign-looking storefront with automation-detection cloaking to a counterfeit QuickBooks sign-in page. The page rewrote Intuit service requests to attacker-controlled subdomains, consistent with an adversary-in-the-middle workflow, though credential theft was not confirmed. Defenders should evaluate attacker-controlled notification content, verify the business request itself, and follow the full navigation chain rather than trusting sender authentication alone.",
