      "report_date": "2026-11-10",
      "title": "How TeamPCP Turned Developer Tools Into a Route for Data Theft",
      "summary": "TeamPCP, a financially motivated group that appeared in late 2025, compromised Aqua Security's Trivy scanner in March 2026 and used the credentials it stole to tamper with other tools, including Checkmarx KICS and LiteLLM. The group then poisoned the Nx Console VS Code extension, and it claims it stole about 3,800 internal GitHub repositories from one employee's device, a figure GitHub called \"directionally consistent\" with its investigation. Its operating sequence was to get into trusted software, harvest every reachable secret, encrypt and exfiltrate the data, maintain access, and then resell or use the credentials, sometimes weeks later. Defenders should pin third-party actions to full commit SHAs, limit secret scope, delay risky updates, and rotate exposed credentials even after malicious packages are removed. The report also argues that monitoring and blocking outbound data transfers is a useful control.",
      "completeness": "Low",
