      "report_date": "2026-11-10",
      "title": "BluePhishing: Initial Access via Third-Party Agent Blueprint Consent",
      "summary": "Proofpoint researchers describe BluePhishing, a proof-of-concept evolution of OAuth consent abuse that targets Microsoft Entra Agent ID. Attackers socially engineer victims into granting consent via a link to a malicious agent blueprint, which creates a blueprint principal in the victim tenant with the implicit AgentIdentity.CreateAsManager permission. The attacker then uses that permission to create agent identities that inherit permissions bundled through requiredResourceAccess and inheritable permissions. Because inherited permissions are hard to audit and agent activity resembles legitimate autonomous behavior, detection is harder, and one blueprint can yield up to 250 agent identities. Defenders should restrict user consent for third-party agents and track the blueprint ID, which is consistent across tenants. Proofpoint has not observed in-the-wild activity.",
      "completeness": "Low",
